      "annotatedScript": "On the second Tuesday of October, the Bitterroot Range in western Montana did not look like a postcard. [PAUSE 1.5s] It looked like wet iron. Sleet slanted sideways through the ponderosa pines, turning the Bear Creek Trailhead into a slick bowl of red clay and pine needles. At eleven-forty at night, fifty-four-year-old Caleb Vance knelt beside the tailgate of his dented pickup truck, lacing his boots with fingers stiff from twenty years of search-and-rescue callouts. [BEAT] Caleb was not a man who owned a tactical vest or a drone. He worked thirty hours a week at a hardware store in Hamilton, and on weekends he taught rookies how to read mud, bent grass, and the weight of a heel print. [SLOW DOWN] His rule was simple: [EMPHASIS: the mountain never lies, but it whispers].\n\nThat night, the mountain had swallowed an eleven-year-old girl named Maya Lin. [PAUSE 1.5s] Six hours earlier, Maya had walked fifty yards from her father's camp to collect dry kindling before the front rolled in. The sleet had dropped visibility to thirty feet in under ten minutes. By the time her father realized she had not come back, the temperature had plunged to twenty-nine degrees, and the wind was gusting thirty-five miles an hour across the granite ridges. [LOWER VOICE] A child in a fleece jacket does not have until morning in twenty-nine-degree sleet. Caleb zipped his waxed canvas coat, clipped a dim amber headlamp to his brow, and walked toward the green canvas command tent glowing like a lantern in the dark.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Inside the tent, three propane heaters hissed against the wet canvas, smelling of kerosene and damp wool. Sheriff Harlan Brooks stood over a folding table covered in laminated topographic maps weighted down with coffee mugs. Twelve volunteers in bright orange parkas crowded around him, waiting for assignments. [SPEED UP] Brooks tapped a red grease-pencil circle over the Bear Creek drainage\u2014a steep, boulder-choked ravine three hundred yards below the camp. \"Lost hikers go downhill,\" Brooks said, his voice hoarse from radio static. \"Water runs down, gravity pulls down, panic pushes down. Every team sweeps the creek bed until zero-two-hundred. If we don't find her by then, we pull back and wait for the Blackhawk with thermal imaging at first light.\"\n\nCaleb stepped to the edge of the table, his wet coat dripping onto the map. [LEAN IN] \"Harlan, the creek bed is a wind tunnel tonight,\" Caleb said quietly. \"If she stepped off the bench, she'd feel that draft immediately. A smart kid might not go down.\" Brooks didn't look up from his clipboard. \"Caleb, I respect your miles, but I've got twelve cold bodies out there and a freezing rain warning. We play the odds. Odds say the creek.\" [BEAT] A younger deputy beside the radio muttered, \"Thermal's up at six anyway. No sense breaking an ankle in the dark for a hunch.\" Caleb said nothing. He knew better than to argue with a man holding a clipboard. Instead, he asked for the last known point\u2014the flat boulder where Maya's father had found a single dropped pine cone\u2014and walked back out into the sleet alone.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] By two-fifteen in the morning, Caleb's headlamp beam was a weak yellow smudge against the white curtain of sleet. He had walked the upper rim of the Bear Creek drainage twice, sweeping his light across mud, granite, and matted beargrass. Nothing. No boot tread. No torn thread of blue fleece. [PAUSE 1.5s] His knees ached with a deep, familiar throb, and the sleet had found the seam at the back of his collar, trickling ice water down his spine. On the radio clipped to his shoulder, the creek-bed teams began checking in one by one. \"Team Two, clear to the falls. Nothing.\" \"Team Four, negative.\" Then Sheriff Brooks's voice crackled through the static: \"All units, stand down. Return to trailhead. Thermal bird launches at oh-six-hundred.\"\n\n[PAUSE 2s] [STILLNESS] Caleb stood motionless under a dripping fir tree. His hand hovered over the radio mic. If he keyed it, he would be warm in twenty minutes, drinking burnt coffee by a propane heater. If he stayed, he was just an old man wandering in the dark against orders. [LOWER VOICE] He thought about Maya's father sitting in the back of a patrol car, staring at the dashboard clock. He thought about how thermal cameras work\u2014how a soaked fleece jacket under dense fir canopy can hide a body's heat signature until it is too late. Caleb unclipped the radio, turned the volume down to a whisper, and clicked his headlamp to its lowest setting so the glare wouldn't blind him to shadows.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] He walked back to the flat boulder where the pine cone had been found. Instead of looking at the ground, he forced himself to stop thinking like a search-and-rescue coordinator and start thinking like an eleven-year-old girl who was suddenly cold and lost. [LEAN IN] He crouched down until his eyes were roughly four feet off the dirt. From there, the world looked completely different. The creek bed below wasn't just dark; it roared like a freight train. Above him, the alder thickets looked like a wall, quiet and sheltered. Caleb swept his dim beam slowly across the edge of the thicket. [PAUSE 1.5s] That was when he saw it.\n\nA single alder twig, no thicker than a pencil lead, snapped clean\u2014[EMPHASIS: forty-two inches off the frozen mud]. [BEAT] Not broken at waist height by a passing deer, and not chewed by an elk. The pale green inner wood was still fresh, gleaming wet in his beam, and the thin brown bark was peeled downward on the uphill side. Caleb leaned closer, his breath fogging the air. \"You grabbed this to pull yourself up,\" he whispered to the empty woods. [SPEED UP] Ten feet higher, behind a mossy deadfall, he found a second twig, snapped at the exact same height, bark peeled downward. Twenty feet beyond that, a third\u2014just a hairline fracture in a huckleberry stem, bent uphill.\n\n[EMPHASIS: Everyone else was looking down], Caleb realized. [EMPHASIS: She was climbing toward the windbreak]. Step by agonizing step, Caleb followed the forty-two-inch trail up the steep granite shoulder, reading tiny fractures no wider than a fingernail. Twice he lost the line on bare rock, knelt in the freezing mud, and waited until his eyes caught the faint pale gleam of a bruised stem. At three-forty in the morning, three hundred yards above the creek bed, his beam caught a hollow beneath the roots of an upturned cedar. Curled inside, wrapped in a soaked blue fleece, was Maya Lin. [PAUSE 2s] Her lips were gray, and she was shivering too hard to speak, but when Caleb knelt beside her and whispered her name, her eyes fluttered open.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] At four-thirty, Caleb walked out of the treeline carrying Maya wrapped in his own waxed canvas coat, her arms locked tight around his neck. Nobody in the command tent cheered. [PAUSE 1.5s] The silence was heavier than that. Sheriff Brooks stood up from the folding table, his clipboard slipping from his fingers and clattering onto the muddy floor. Maya's father collapsed to his knees in the wet clay, sobbing into his daughter's hair as paramedics wrapped her in foil blankets. [WARM SMILE] Caleb didn't wait for the debrief. He retrieved his coat, walked back to his dented pickup, and sat behind the wheel for ten minutes with the heater blasting, watching his own hands shake.\n\n[STILLNESS] Maya Lin recovered completely. Six months later, a small package arrived at the hardware store in Hamilton\u2014a framed pencil drawing of a cedar tree, with three tiny snapped twigs drawn carefully at the bottom. [LOWER VOICE] Caleb hung it behind the paint counter, right next to the key-cutting machine. Every time someone asks him how he found a lost child in a sleet storm when twelve trained teams and a sheriff's department couldn't, Caleb gives the same answer. [PAUSE 1.5s] He says, \"I didn't do anything magical. I just stopped looking where everyone else was looking, and I knelt down to forty-two inches.\"",
