      "annotatedScript": "[SLOW DOWN] At five-forty on a November morning in Toledo, Ohio, the lake-effect rain fell sideways against the corrugated siding of Halstead Stamping & Forge. [BEAT] Elena Vance sat in her rented sedan with the defrost blasting, watching the sodium-vapor lamps turn the puddles in the employee lot into pools of amber oil. She was forty-four years old, sixteen years into a career as an independent industrial safety trainer, and she had a laminated binder on the passenger seat that smelled faintly of dry-erase markers and motel coffee.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Inside, the plant was already awake. [PAUSE 1.5s] The air tasted of hot steel, cutting fluid, and ozone. Overhead, the crane rails hummed. Elena followed the plant manager, a brisk man named Marcus Bell, past three rows of stamping presses until they stopped beside Bay Four. A four-hundred-ton hydraulic press sat there like a sleeping iron beast, its yellow safety curtain dusty and its floor mat curled at the corners.\n\n\"Three near-misses since March,\" Marcus said, not looking at the machine. He tapped his watch. \"And a crushed index finger in August. Corporate wants a full re-certification by Friday. You've got forty-five minutes with the day shift in the breakroom at six.\"\n\nElena looked at the press. [LOWER VOICE] Beneath the control box, wedged tightly behind the left-hand palm button, was a two-inch scrap of oak. Someone had jammed the interlock so the operator could run the press one-handed. [PAUSE 2s] [EMPHASIS: Someone had chosen speed over a finger.]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At six o'clock, twenty-two operators filed into the breakroom. They smelled of tobacco and damp wool. They sat on folding chairs with their arms crossed, staring at the projector screen Elena had set up beside the vending machines. She clicked to her first slide: a bar chart of regional hand injuries.\n\n[SPEED UP] She got through three slides before a man in the back row spoke up. His name was Ray Kowalski. Fifty-eight years old, thirty-one years on the floor, knuckles scarred white from a lifetime of sheet metal.\n\n\"Excuse me, ma'am,\" Ray said. His voice wasn't loud, just heavy. \"Did Marcus tell you they raised our quota fourteen percent after the August audit?\"\n\nElena paused with the clicker in her hand. \"No,\" she said. \"He didn't.\"\n\n\"Using both palm buttons adds [EMPHASIS: four seconds per cycle],\" Ray said, leaning forward. \"Four seconds. Times four hundred parts a shift. That's twenty-six minutes we don't have. If we miss quota, we lose the team bonus. If we lose the team bonus, my kid's braces don't get paid for. So you can show us all the red triangles you want. Tomorrow morning, the oak block goes back in.\"\n\n[PAUSE 2s] The room went dead quiet except for the hum of the soda machine. Elena looked at twenty-two pairs of eyes. None of them were angry at her. They were just tired of being lectured by people who got to go home with clean fingernails.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] That night, in a motel room off Interstate 75, Elena couldn't sleep. The radiator clanked. She sat at the tiny laminate desk under a flickering bulb, her binder open to the attendance sheet. Twenty-two signatures. Every box checked. Legally, Halstead Stamping & Forge was covered. If another hand got caught in Bay Four on Thursday, the company could pull out that sheet of paper and say, [LOWER VOICE] \"We trained them. They signed.\"\n\n[BEAT] Elena stared at her own handwriting in the margins. For sixteen years, she had believed she was saving people. Now, in the fluorescent quiet of a cheap motel, a colder truth settled over her: she had been performing [EMPHASIS: compliance theater]. She was the person companies hired to wash their hands before the blood dried.\n\nAt two-fourteen in the morning, her phone buzzed on the nightstand. It was Marcus.\n\n\"Third shift,\" he said, his voice tight. \"Operator at Bay Four caught his sleeve on the unguarded feed roller. Pulled him halfway to the die before he hit the knee bar. He's shaken up, no broken bones, but the union steward just shut the bay down.\"\n\n[PAUSE 2s] Elena sat on the edge of the mattress, staring at the rain streaking the window. She could pack her bag, drive to the airport, and send an invoice. Nobody would blame her. Instead, she reached for her boots.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At five-fifty the next morning, [EMPHASIS: she left the binder in the car]. She walked onto the floor in her steel-toes, bypassed the breakroom, and found Ray Kowalski standing beside the dark silhouette of Bay Four. Yellow tape stretched across the feed roller.\n\n\"You're early,\" Ray said, squinting at her.\n\n\"Put me on the press,\" Elena said.\n\nRay blinked. \"What?\"\n\n\"I want to run ten cycles. Cold. Power off. Show me where you stand.\"\n\nRay hesitated, then tossed her a pair of spare Kevlar gloves. Elena stepped onto the anti-fatigue mat. She reached up for the two-hand palm buttons\u2014and stopped. [LEAN IN] The control box was mounted at the height of her collarbone. To press both buttons simultaneously, she had to hunch her shoulders, lock her elbows, and crane her neck to see the die.\n\n\"How tall was the guy who installed this?\" she asked.\n\n\"Six-two,\" Ray muttered. \"Maintenance lead. Retired last year.\"\n\nElena looked around the bay. Not a single operator on the day shift was over five-ten. Every time they ran a part, they were fighting the machine's geometry. No wonder they jammed the left button with oak. It wasn't laziness. [PAUSE 1.5s] It was survival.\n\n[SPEED UP] Ten minutes later, Marcus Bell hurried onto the floor, his tie crooked. \"Elena, the corporate auditor is on the line. He needs your sign-off on the re-certification.\"\n\nElena didn't look at him. She reached into her jacket pocket, pulled out a stick of white soapstone chalk, and knelt on the oil-stained concrete. [STILLNESS] Slowly, deliberately, she drew a thick white line three feet back from Bay Four.\n\n\"I'm not signing,\" she said, standing up and dusting her hands. \"Not today.\"\n\nMarcus's face flushed. \"You're under contract.\"\n\n\"My contract says I certify a safe floor,\" Elena said, her voice steady enough to carry over the nearby presses. \"This floor isn't safe. Lower the control pedestal six inches, put a hinged guard on the feed roller, and reset the cycle quota to match the actual two-hand timing. Do that, and I'll sign it for free.\"\n\n[PAUSE 2s] Ray Kowalski looked at the white chalk line, then at Elena. Slowly, he walked over to the control box, pulled the two-inch oak block out from behind the palm button, and dropped it into the scrap bin.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] Six months later, Elena was at an airport gate in Chicago when her phone chimed with a text from an unfamiliar number. It was a photograph. Bay Four was gleaming under new LEDs. The control pedestal sat six inches lower. And there, on the concrete floor, sealed beneath a fresh coat of industrial clearcoat so the forklifts wouldn't wear it away, was [EMPHASIS: the white chalk line].\n\nBelow the photo, three words from Ray: [WARM SMILE] \"One hundred eighty days.\"\n\n[PAUSE 2s] [LOWER VOICE] Safety didn't start when Elena walked into the breakroom with a slide deck. It started the moment she had the courage to [EMPHASIS: stand where the work happens]\u2014and refuse to sign a lie.",
