      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] At five-fifteen on a Tuesday morning in May, the air inside the tin hangar at The Pas smelled of avgas, wet cedar, and cold aluminum. [BEAT] Callum Mercer stood beside the port float of his de Havilland Beaver, a sixty-year-old bush plane with patched fabric on the rudder and thirty-four thousand hours in its logbooks. Outside, the Saskatchewan River had swollen past its winter levees, swallowing the gravel roads and turning a hundred miles of northern spruce into a brown, moving sea. [EMPHASIS: The river had forgotten where its banks were.] [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nCallum was fifty-eight, with a silver stubble he had not shaved since Sunday and knuckles permanently stained from checking oil dipsticks in sub-zero wind. Every morning for nineteen years, he had flown mail, groceries, and clinic nurses into the fly-in communities north of the fifty-fourth parallel. Usually, the job was rhythm and math: weight and balance, fuel burn, wind shear over the treeline. [BEAT] That morning, the math had changed. Three days of warm rain on top of rotting river ice had washed out the winter road to Red Sucker Lake, and the nursing station's backup generator had flooded out at midnight. Inside the Beaver's cabin sat three insulated coolers packed with [EMPHASIS: forty-two pounds of insulin and antibiotics], strapped down with nylon webbing behind the co-pilot seat. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SPEED UP] Ray Pelletier, the sixty-two-year-old dispatcher whose knees clicked when he walked, came out of the shack with a clipboard and a grim mouth. \"Ceiling at Norway House is four hundred feet and dropping,\" Ray said, tapping the paper with a stubby pencil. \"Freezing drizzle at two thousand. You can't climb over it, Cal. You'd ice up before you cleared the marsh.\" Callum pulled his fleece collar up and looked toward the northeast, where the sky hung like wet wool. \"If I stay under four hundred,\" Callum said, \"I'm flying the river bends.\" Ray shook his head. \"The river bends are full of floating timber. One deadhead spruce through a pontoon, and you're swimming in forty-degree water. Wait until noon.\" [BEAT] Callum looked at the coolers through the cabin window. \"Dr. Okafor said the clinic's fridge hit fifty-two degrees at four a.m. Noon is too late for the insulin.\" [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] He took off at five-forty-two, the Beaver's radial engine rattling the hangar's corrugated roof as the floats broke suction from the flooded bay. [LEAN IN] For the first forty minutes, the world was a gray tunnel. Callum kept the altimeter needle pinned at three hundred feet, watching the submerged treetops slide beneath his belly like dark teeth. Rain hammered the windshield so hard the wipers could barely smear it clear. Every ten minutes, he tapped the side of the compass housing to make sure the needle wasn't sticking. [BEAT] Then, twenty miles south of Red Sucker Lake, the ceiling dropped again. Mist curled over the spruce ridges, sealing the valley ahead. Callum banked left to follow the wider channel, and that was when the oil-temperature gauge began to climb. [LOWER VOICE] Not fast. Just a slow, stubborn creep toward the red line. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[STILLNESS] He had a choice that every bush pilot eventually meets in the dark: turn back with the cargo intact and let someone else's clock run out, or push into a closing box with a warming engine. [BEAT] Callum reached for the mic, keyed the frequency for Red Sucker Lake's nursing station, and heard only the hiss of static. He tried again. Nothing. The repeater tower on the ridge must have lost its solar bank in the storm. [LOWER VOICE] He was alone at three hundred feet, twenty miles from a flooded dock, with twenty-two minutes of safe oil pressure left and a plane that could not climb into the clouds. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Callum's hands tightened on the yoke until his forearms burned. His father had been a bush pilot in the seventies, a man who had taught him one rule above all others: [EMPHASIS: you do not fly the weather you wish you had.] Turning back meant saving the airframe, saving his license, saving his own skin. Nobody at Transport Canada would fault him. Ray Pelletier would pour him coffee and nod. [BEAT] Yet in Callum's mind, he could see Dr. Hannah Okafor standing in a dark clinic hallway with a flashlight, checking the vials for a seven-year-old boy whose name he didn't even know. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] He didn't climb. Instead, he did something that looked, from the ground, like madness. He dropped the Beaver down to a hundred feet, then fifty, skimming just above the drowned alder thickets where the wind was calmer and the mist thinned against the water. [SPEED UP] At that height, there is no room for a mistake. A single gust, a single submerged power line, and the story ends in two seconds. Callum trimmed the nose slightly up, leaned forward until his forehead nearly touched the dash, and flew by the color of the water\u2014dark brown meant deep channel; pale green meant shallow shoal; white foam meant a snag. [BEAT] Ten minutes. Twelve minutes. The oil temperature touched the red line. The engine coughed once, a dry metallic shudder that ran straight through the rudder pedals into his boots. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[LOWER VOICE] Ahead, the mist parted just enough to show the bay at Red Sucker Lake. The wooden dock was gone\u2014completely underwater. Floating logs choked the shoreline. There was nowhere safe to set a floatplane down. [BEAT] Callum banked in a tight, slow circle, burning precious seconds, searching for a strip of open water. And then he saw them. [WARM SMILE] Out past the flooded dock, bobbing in the gray chop, were [EMPHASIS: two Coleman lanterns tied to aluminum canoes], anchored fifty yards apart to mark a clear lane between the deadheads. Someone had heard the radial engine through the fog and paddled out in freezing rain to build him a runway out of light. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Callum lined up between the two lanterns. He chopped the throttle, pulled the yoke back into his lap, and held the Beaver [EMPHASIS: twelve feet above the alder] until the airspeed bled away. The floats kissed the water with a long, hissing spray, skipped once, and settled into the channel. [BEAT] He taxied up to the nearer canoe, where a young man in a yellow slicker caught the strut with a gloved hand. Two minutes later, Dr. Okafor was wading knee-deep in the shallows, lifting the three coolers onto her shoulder without a word. [STILLNESS] She looked up at Callum in the cockpit, rain streaming down her face, and simply pressed her palm against the cold aluminum of the door. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] Callum spent the night on a cot in the clinic's waiting room while the generator hummed back to life on borrowed fuel. The next morning, the sky cleared to a sharp, impossible blue. A mechanic flew in from Thompson with a spare oil cooler, and by noon the Beaver was back in the air. [BEAT] Nobody wrote a newspaper column about it. There was no medal, no press conference, no plaque on the hangar wall. [WARM SMILE] Yet every spring after that, whenever the ice broke early and the rivers rose, the people of Red Sucker Lake kept two Coleman lanterns fueled and waiting in the boathouse\u2014just in case a silver-haired pilot came looking for a place to land. [PAUSE 2s]",
