      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] On the third Thursday of November, at ten-forty at night, Nadia Kowalski was sitting in a windowless basement room beneath Market Street in Wheeling, West Virginia, listening to the rain hit the sidewalk grates above her head. [PAUSE 1.5s] Nadia was thirty-four, the overnight producer for WBRV 91.3 FM, a fifty-thousand-watt public radio station that occupied two rented rooms below a dry cleaner and a closed hardware store. The studio smelled of warm vacuum tubes, damp concrete, and the burnt-pecan coffee Nadia kept in a dented thermos beside the mixing console. [BEAT] Her routine on Thursday nights was almost mechanical. She cued the syndicated jazz hour, logged the transmitter readings on a yellow legal pad, checked the National Weather Service teletype, and waited for the midnight news feed from Charleston. [SLOW DOWN] Outside, however, Wheeling Creek had been rising since noon. Three days of warm rain over frozen ground had turned every hollow in Ohio County into a chute. By eleven o'clock, the storm drains at the foot of Market Street stopped taking water and began pushing it back up through the grates. [LEAN IN] Nadia noticed it first not by looking down, but by listening. The low hum of the studio air handler changed pitch\u2014a wet, muffled slurp beneath the floorboards. When she glanced past the edge of the oak desk, a thin black ribbon of creek water was already sliding across the linoleum toward the base of the equipment rack. [PAUSE 1.5s] If that water reached the main power strip, the station would drop off the air, and WBRV was the only local signal still broadcasting county road closures to the east-side neighborhoods.\n\n[SPEED UP] Nadia stood up, grabbed a stack of promotional tote bags from the supply shelf, and wedged them along the gap under the stairwell door. It took four minutes for the water to soak straight through the canvas and keep coming. [BEAT] She reached for the wall phone and dialed the home number of Arthur Vance, the station's sixty-two-year-old chief engineer. On the fourth ring, Arthur answered, his voice thick with sleep. \"Nadia? What's wrong?\" [LOWER VOICE] \"The creek is in the basement, Arthur,\" Nadia said, watching the water touch the rubber casters of her chair. \"It's about an inch deep at the door and moving toward the rack.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] On the other end of the line, she heard a sharp intake of breath and the rustle of sheets. \"Listen to me carefully,\" Arthur said. \"Do not touch the rack. Do not try to sandbag it. [EMPHASIS: pull the main breaker and get out] before it hits the junction box.\" [BEAT] \"Arthur, the county sheriff's office just sent a bulletin,\" Nadia replied, her eyes fixed on the glowing amber meters of the console. \"The Fulton Bridge approach is washed out. If the board goes dark, nobody knows the bridge is out.\" \"Nadia, that's a four-hundred-volt feed behind the wall,\" Arthur snapped. \"A radio signal isn't worth your life. Pull the breaker, grab your coat, and walk up the stairs.\" [PAUSE 2s] She hung up the receiver slowly. The water was now two inches deep, cold enough to numb her ankles through her wool socks. She looked at the breaker panel on the far wall, then back at the microphone arm, and realized she had not even turned on the studio monitor to hear what was going out over the air.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By eleven-thirty, the jazz hour had ended and the automated station ID was looping every ninety seconds because the satellite dish on the roof had lost its lock in the sleet. [SLOW DOWN] Nadia stood on top of a milk crate behind the console, her knees aching, her hands shaking from cold and adrenaline. The water had reached four inches. An empty coffee cup floated past her left ankle and bumped gently against the base of the equipment rack. [BEAT] Down the hall, the sump pump gave a violent metallic cough and died. The basement lights flickered once, dimmed to a bruised yellow, and held. [LOWER VOICE] Nadia stared at the red \"ON AIR\" lamp above the door. Every instinct she had\u2014every safety manual, Arthur's voice, the cold creeping up her shins\u2014told her to flip the main switch and climb the concrete steps to Market Street. She was a producer, not a firefighter. Nobody had hired her to stand in four inches of river water in the dark. [PAUSE 1.5s] Then the studio phone rang. She almost didn't answer it. When she picked up the handset, a woman's voice, tight with panic, came through the static. \"Is anybody there?\" the caller asked. \"My husband is driving home from the night shift at the plant on Route 40. He always takes the Fulton Bridge. Does he know it's closed?\" [PAUSE 2s] Nadia looked at the weather teletype. The county's emergency alert system had failed twenty minutes earlier when the courthouse repeater lost power. [EMPHASIS: the only signal left on the dial] was the one humming right in front of her.\n\n[STILLNESS] Nadia set the phone down without hanging it up, stepped off the milk crate into the freezing water, and pulled a wooden folding chair onto the heavy rubber mat beneath the desk. She climbed onto the chair so her feet were clear of the floor, leaned forward, and opened the live microphone channel. [PAUSE 1.5s] \"This is Nadia Kowalski at WBRV 91.3,\" she said, her voice steadier than she expected. \"If you are listening in your car on Route 40 or heading toward the east side, do not take the Fulton Bridge. The approach is underwater.\" [BEAT] Five minutes later, Arthur Vance's pickup truck skidded to a stop outside the dry cleaner upstairs. He came down the concrete steps in chest waders, carrying a heavy-duty marine battery and a flashlight clamped between his teeth. He stopped at the bottom step, saw Nadia perched on the folding chair with [EMPHASIS: dry socks and a live mic], and didn't say a word about the breaker. [WARM SMILE] Instead, he waded straight to the equipment rack, unbolted the lowest power strip, and lifted it onto a wooden shelf four feet off the floor. \"You're stubborn,\" Arthur muttered, wiping rain from his glasses. \"How long can we hold it?\" Nadia asked, keeping one eye on the clock. \"Until the river hits the console,\" Arthur said. \"Read the bulletins. I'll watch the rack.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] For the next two hours, Nadia read every road closure, every shelter address, and every power-outage update that came across the sheriff's dispatch radio Arthur had brought from his truck. Between bulletins, she didn't play music. She just talked\u2014quietly, clearly, naming streets and landmarks so drivers in the dark would know someone was awake with them.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At two-fifteen in the morning, the creek crested at six inches inside the basement, half an inch below the bottom edge of the mixing board, and began to recede. [SLOW DOWN] By four-thirty, the state police had barricaded both ends of the Fulton Bridge, and the morning host arrived upstairs with two thermoses of hot soup and a pair of dry boots. [PAUSE 1.5s] Nadia finally switched the board to the automated feed, climbed down from the folding chair, and walked up the stairs into the cold, grey dawn. Three days later, when the station's signal was still being praised at the county commission meeting, a man in a muddy utility jacket walked into the temporary upstairs office and asked for the woman who had been on the air Thursday night. [LEAN IN] He didn't give his name. He just told Nadia that he had been three hundred yards from the Fulton Bridge washout in the dark, with his headlights barely cutting the rain, when her voice came through his dashboard speaker and told him to turn around. [PAUSE 2s] [LOWER VOICE] The basement studio was gutted and rebuilt six months later, two feet higher on steel risers. But whenever a storm warning crosses the teletype in Wheeling, the overnight producer still checks the rubber mat under the desk first, remembering the night a voice in the dark kept the whole town from driving into the river\u2014[EMPHASIS: still here, still on the air].",
